          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What actually happens in the airways during an asthma flare-up?",
          "answer": "The sources I searched only partly answer this. They describe asthma in general terms but do not explain what happens in the airways during a flare-up.\n\nFrom the General clinical reference \"Asthma\" summary, the parts I can confirm are:\n\n- **Inflammation:** The airway lining becomes inflamed.\n- **Narrowing:** The muscle around the airways is prone to tighten, which narrows them. This is the airflow obstruction.\n- **Variability:** The narrowing changes from day to day and within a day, and it is at least partly reversible. This is what sets asthma apart from COPD, which stays persistent.\n- **Typical signs:** Wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough, often worse at night or in the early morning, and often set off by triggers.\n\nThe sources I retrieved do not describe mucus production or airway remodeling in asthma, so I cannot explain those here. I also could not find a passage that walks through the sequence of events in a flare-up.\n\nA guidance passage on asthma treatment notes that inflammation is the underlying problem that treatment targets, which fits the general picture above. It does not give the detailed mechanisms.\n\nIf you want to go further, a clinician or a respiratory specialist can explain how mucus and remodeling fit into a person's own asthma. If breathing becomes hard, gets worse quickly, or you cannot speak in full sentences, seek urgent medical care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
